# \# Audio anti-spoofing — deepfake and replay detection

# 

# A biometric authentication component that flags spoofed speech before it reaches a speaker verification system. Built on AASIST-L and evaluated against ASVspoof 2019, it covers two attack surfaces: synthesized (deepfake) audio from the LA partition and physical replay attacks from the PA partition.

# 

# \---

# 

# \## What it does

# 

# Speaker verification systems can be bypassed with either a generated voice clone or a recording played back through a speaker. This system sits in front of the verifier and rejects both:

# 

# \- \*\*Logical access (LA):\*\* TTS and voice conversion attacks (A01–A19). The model learns spectro-temporal artifacts left by neural vocoders, waveform concatenation, and voice conversion pipelines.

# \- \*\*Physical access (PA):\*\* Replay attacks across 9 room/device configurations (AA–CC). The model detects the acoustic signature of re-recorded speech.

# 

# A single AASIST-L checkpoint handles both partitions. The output is a scalar P(bonafide) score; a threshold set at the EER point determines accept/reject.

# 

# \---

# 

# \## Results

# 

# \### LA — deepfake detection

# 

# | Split | EER    | min t-DCF |

# |-------|--------|-----------|

# | Dev   | 1.78%  | 0.0236    |

# | Eval  | 11.45% | 0.1353    |

# 

# Dev score separation (bonafide vs. spoof mean) is 0.82. On eval, the model scores A07 (vocoder-based TTS) at 1.6% EER and struggles most with A15 (neural TTS) at 34.4%. The 1.78% → 11.45% gap reflects the unseen attack condition: eval attacks A07–A19 do not appear in training.

# 

# \### PA — replay detection

# 

# | Split | EER    |

# |-------|--------|

# | Dev   | 16.33% |

# | Eval  | 18.73% |

# 

# The hardest replay configuration is AA (small room, close mic, HQ device) at 36–38% EER. Far-mic, low-quality configurations (AC, BC, CC) reach 2–5% EER — the acoustic degradation makes replay easier to detect than close-mic, high-fidelity recordings.

# 

# \---

# 

# \## Architecture

# 

# \*\*Model:\*\* AASIST-L (\~30K parameters), the lightweight variant of AASIST (Audio Anti-Spoofing using Integrated Spectro-Temporal Graph Attention Networks).

# 

# \*\*Pipeline:\*\*

# 

# ```

# Raw waveform (16 kHz, 64,600 samples)

# &#x20; → SincConv front-end (learnable bandpass filters)

# &#x20; → Dual spectral + temporal encoder branches

# &#x20; → Graph attention network (integrates both branches)

# &#x20; → Binary classifier → P(bonafide)

# ```

# 

# SincConv operates directly on the waveform — no mel spectrogram extraction. The graph attention layer integrates spectral and temporal representations before the final linear classifier.

# 

# \---

# 

# \## Project structure

# 

# ```

# phase1/

# ├── config.py               # Paths, hyperparameters, attack type map

# ├── protocol\_parser.py      # ASVspoof CM protocol → DataFrame

# ├── preprocess\_audio.py     # Resample .flac → 16 kHz .npy

# ├── dataset.py              # PyTorch Dataset classes (raw + preprocessed)

# ├── dataset\_memory.py       # In-memory variant (preloads all .npy to RAM)

# ├── aasist\_model.py         # AASIST and AASIST-L implementation

# ├── train.py                # Training loop, EER + t-DCF computation

# ├── evaluate.py             # Inference, per-attack breakdown, plots

# ├── run\_phase1\_test.py      # Integration test suite (4 tests, \~100 samples)

# └── requirements.txt

# ```

# 

# \---

# 

# \## Dataset

# 

# ASVspoof 2019 — two partitions used:

# 

# \*\*LA (Logical Access) — deepfake audio\*\*

# 

# | Split | Total  | Bonafide | Spoof  | Attack types      |

# |-------|--------|----------|--------|-------------------|

# | Train | 25,380 | 2,580    | 22,800 | A01–A06           |

# | Dev   | 24,844 | 2,548    | 22,296 | A01–A06           |

# | Eval  | 71,237 | 7,355    | 63,882 | A07–A19 (unseen)  |

# 

# \*\*PA (Physical Access) — replay attacks\*\*

# 

# | Split | Total  | Bonafide | Spoof  | Configurations |

# |-------|--------|----------|--------|----------------|

# | Dev   | 15,434 | 3,219    | 12,215 | AA–CC          |

# | Eval  | 67,987 | 10,746   | 57,241 | AA–CC          |

# 

# PA configurations combine three room sizes (small/medium/large), mic distance (close/mid/far), and device quality (HQ/LQ). Download from the \[ASVspoof 2019 challenge page](https://datashare.ed.ac.uk/handle/10283/3336).

# 

# \---

# 

# \## Setup

# 

# ```bash

# pip install -r requirements.txt

# ```

# 

# Set `BASE\_DIR` in `config.py` to your LA dataset root:

# 

# ```python

# BASE\_DIR = Path("/path/to/ASVspoof2019/LA")

# ```

# 

# For PA, set `PA\_BASE\_DIR` similarly.

# 

# \---

# 

# \## Usage

# 

# \### 1. Run integration tests (no preprocessing needed)

# 

# ```bash

# python run\_phase1\_test.py

# ```

# 

# Runs 4 tests on \~100 samples: protocol parsing, AASIST forward pass, 5-batch mini training loop, and dev-set inference with an untrained model.

# 

# \### 2. Preprocess audio

# 

# ```bash

# \# All splits

# python preprocess\_audio.py

# 

# \# Quick experiment subset

# python preprocess\_audio.py --subset 1000

# 

# \# Single split, parallel workers

# python preprocess\_audio.py --split train --workers 8

# ```

# 

# Audio is resampled to 16 kHz, normalized to 64,600 samples (4.04s), and saved as `.npy`. Short utterances tile to fill the target length; long utterances truncate.

# 

# \### 3. Train

# 

# ```bash

# python train.py --variant AASIST-L --epochs 10 --batch-size 24

# ```

# 

# Use `AASIST` if you have GPU headroom (full model, \~300K parameters). `AASIST-L` trains comfortably on a single consumer GPU.

# 

# \### 4. Evaluate

# 

# ```bash

# \# Dev set

# python evaluate.py --checkpoint checkpoints/best\_model.pt --split dev --plot

# 

# \# Eval set

# python evaluate.py --checkpoint checkpoints/best\_model.pt --split eval --plot

# ```

# 

# Outputs: overall EER and min t-DCF, per-attack EER bar chart, score distribution histogram, DET curve, and a score file compatible with the official ASVspoof evaluation tools.

# 

# \---

# 

# \## Memory-efficient training

# 

# `dataset\_memory.py` provides `ASVspoofMemoryDataset`, which preloads all `.npy` files into RAM at init. This eliminates per-batch disk I/O entirely. Use it when disk I/O is the bottleneck:

# 

# ```python

# from dataset\_memory import ASVspoofMemoryDataset

# 

# train\_ds = ASVspoofMemoryDataset(

# &#x20;   metadata\_csv="metadata/train.csv",

# &#x20;   npy\_dir="preprocessed/train"

# )

# ```

# 

# The full training set (\~25K utterances at 64,600 samples each, float32) requires approximately 6.5 GB of RAM.

# 

# \---

# 

# \## Known limitations

# 

# \*\*Unseen attack generalization:\*\* The 1.78% dev EER vs. 11.45% eval EER gap is expected — the model has never seen A07–A19 attack types during training. Data augmentation with synthesized attacks or domain adaptation closes this gap in published work.

# 

# \*\*PA close-mic HQ configurations (AA, BA, CA):\*\* EER stays above 22% for all three. The replayed audio is acoustically close to live speech and gives the model limited signal to exploit. Training on PA-specific data or using channel-aware features addresses this.

# 

# \*\*Training curve:\*\* Dev loss plateaus around 0.69 while train loss drops to \~0.42, indicating the model is not generalizing on the dev partition during training. The EER metric tracks better than the loss here and is the reliable indicator of model quality.

# 

# \*\*Single modality:\*\* The system uses audio only. A full anti-spoofing stack would fuse with face liveness detection for multimodal rejection.

# 

# \---

# 

# \## Troubleshooting

# 

# \*\*FileNotFoundError on protocol files:\*\* Check the exact filenames in `ASVspoof2019\_LA\_cm\_protocols/` and update the paths in `config.py`.

# 

# \*\*Audio loading errors:\*\* Ensure `libsndfile` is installed (`pip install soundfile`; on Linux: `sudo apt install libsndfile1`).

# 

# \*\*CUDA out of memory:\*\* Switch to `AASIST-L` or reduce batch size. Phase 1 integration tests run fine on CPU.

